 ## Changelog
+- 2026-10-16: Voxel LoD, Voxy style (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +4 tests `lod::tests::{a_column_is_its_top_block_and_the_water_over_it, a_cell_keeps_the_highest_child_and_a_bay_stays_water, water_is_darker_the_deeper_it_is, a_tile_meshes_to_cells_and_the_ring_cuts_a_hole}`, run them first with `--release`). Replaces the smooth heightfield LoD (`Tectonic::heights`, one flat colour per vertex, Tectonic world only); `lod: Some(..)` for both world types.
+    - Why the old one looked bad (screenshots): it was a different surface from the chunks (4 / 8 / 16 block grid, colours made up from height and slope, no tile, no tint, no trees, `light` from the slope) and joined to them by a 56 block hole inside a 64 block ring (an 8 block overlap of two unrelated surfaces, then an unlit gap in the water). Voxy keeps the real blocks of far sections and meshes every mip level with the block's own model; that is what the drone view shows.
+    - Now: `Store` (worker thread `lod`, own `OverworldGenerator` + `WorldChunkManager`, `.tectonic()` as the world type says) makes a tile's 4 x 4 chunks (`generate`, and `populate` on 2 x 2 blocks in raster order, one chunk of margin on the -x / -z side, for tiles within `TREES` = 300 blocks of the eye) and keeps one `Col` per column (top block, its meta and biome tint, the water over it). `resolve` = `Mipper.mip` for a cell of `step` x `step` columns: the highest surface wins (tree tops and peaks survive), a mostly-water shore cell stays water. `build` meshes a tile at step 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 (`step_for`: <120, <200, <330, <520, else; tiles are 64 blocks): top face with the block's real tile and tint, a wall to every lower neighbour (cells outside the tile are read from the neighbour tile's columns) in segments one cell tall, top block's side tile first then dirt / stone (`wall_block`), edge walls hang an extra `2 x step` (replaces the skirt), water as one flat sheet whose colour is the floor lit through the water mixed with the water tile at alpha 138 (`water_rgb`).
+    - Blend: same atlas, tint, face shades (1.0 / 0.8 / 0.6), light 15 and fog as the chunks. `Tile::cut` removes the cells whose top block's section `world::chunks::in_range` says the ring draws (eye quantised to 2 blocks, land cut 8 blocks inside the range, water 2.5: translucent real water over an opaque LoD sheet would show twice), and what stays under the real chunks is `EPS` = 0.04 lower with walls `EPS` inside the cell, so the real surface wins the depth test and the two never z-fight.
+    - Needed: `mesh::tint_of` and `chunks::biome_tints` are `pub(crate)`; `Lod::new(seed, tectonic)`; `Lod::update(device, eye, rh, rv, in_frustum)`.
+    - ponytail: no persistence (tiles are made again every run, nearest first: a few seconds to a minute on a phone for the whole 768 radius, the ring is covered first); a cell is its top block over its whole footprint, no overhangs, caves or interiors; the player's edits do not reach the LoD; trees only near (beyond 300 blocks a forest is lawn); one worker; ~1 MB of GPU buffers per tile at step 1. UNVERIFIED: that it compiles, speed and memory on a device, how the mip rule looks on Tectonic's mountains, the water colour against the real sheet, the distances. Levers: `step_for`, `TREES`, `RADIUS`, `TILE`, the two slacks. Not done (Voxy has them): saving the LoD, ingesting real chunks as they unload, a coarser far tree (leaf-mass) model.
 - 2026-10-15: M22 minecarts (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +7 tests `minecart::tests::*`, +the minecart and furnace-cart recipes). New `world/minecart.rs` ports `EntityMinecart.onUpdate` (gravity, the cell and the rail below, `func_514_g` rail point, slope pull 1/128, speed turned along the rail's shape, off powered rail halves it or stops it under 0.03, on one adds 0.06, position pulled onto the rail's line, `moveEntity`, slope end snap, rider x0.75 then x0.997, furnace push / fuel (1 in 4 ticks while pushing, +1200 per coal), drag 0.96, speed change from the height difference, cell change redirects the motion, yaw flip near a half turn), `applyEntityCollision` (with the `prevPosX` quirk), `attackEntityFrom` (cart + furnace drop), `interact`, `ItemMinecart`. `lib.rs`: tap on a cart (empty hand rides an empty cart and pushes a furnace cart; coal fuels it; an item hits it; nearest of mob, boat and cart wins), jump gets off, the rider follows the cart, `ItemMinecart.onItemUse` on a rail, detector rails (`28`) go on while a cart's box is over their cell and off when it is not (kept in `detectors`, so a rail left on across a reload stays on until a cart passes), `redstone`: rail 28 is a power source (`isPoweringTo` everywhere, `isIndirectlyPoweringTo` up); `render/items.rs`: `push_cart`; `boat::sweep` is the shared 5 cm move; `Effects::puff` for the cart's smoke. ponytail: no chest cart, mobs do not board, no pitch on slopes, carts are not saved, `largesmoke` is an ordinary puff.
 - 2026-10-15: FastNoise port (FastNoise-dev = zfastnoise, zfastsurface, zmatcomp, zfastnoise-ocl). Tested in the Linux scratch crate (rustc 1.75, `world/biome` + `world/gen` minus `populate`): `matches_java_reference` (18 golden chunks) and the other 6 tests pass, so parity with the b1.7.3 Java holds. The mod rewrites `populateNoise`, `populateBiomes` and `SurfaceBuilder` for 1.21 palette storage; the mc-rs counterparts are `generate_terrain`, `load_block_generator_data` and `replace_blocks_for_biome`.
     - Ported: reduced allocations (`OctaveNoise::generate_into` / `generate_2d_into` fill a caller's buffer; `column` fills the cached column in place and the surface pass keeps its three 16x16 noise grids on the stack: 8 heap allocations less per chunk plus 5 per cached column) and FastSurface's packed column walk (`replace_blocks_for_biome` works on one 128-block column slice, no per-block index arithmetic or bounds checks, the `Random` is still drawn for every y). Measured on 400 chunks, release: `generate` ~1.6-1.9 ms -> ~1.55 ms, terrain 1.2-1.4 -> 1.16 ms, surface 150-190 -> 131 us; the machine is noisy, so read it as a few percent up to ~10%.
